<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<PathAssistant xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        The BUY-SIDE LOI path, for the Acquisition_LOI record type.
        Five steps: Draft -> Prepare/Review -> Sent -> Counter -> Completed.

        EVERY <info> TEXT BELOW IS COPIED BYTE-IDENTICAL FROM LOI_Path, the __MASTER__ path
        this file supersedes (now deactivated, retained as the record of that text). Nothing on
        the acquisition side is reworded, re-ordered, added or dropped by this tranche. The
        absence of <fieldNames> on every step is ALSO carried forward deliberately - LOI_Path
        declared none, and adding key fields here would change what an acquisition user sees on
        a live Path for no requested reason.

        WHY A NEW FILE AT ALL. A Path is configured PER RECORD TYPE and recordTypeName cannot be
        re-pointed on an existing PathAssistant, so a __MASTER__ path cannot simply be adopted
        by a new record type. Same shape as Tranche 2's Disposition_Path split and 3A's NDA_Path
        split.

        STEP ORDER IS ENFORCED BY THE MASTER VALUE SET, NOT BY THIS FILE. Runtime display order
        comes from Stage__c's picklist value order (sorted=false) filtered by this record type;
        the order in this file is only for readability. The five DISPOSITION values were
        APPENDED to Stage__c after the five acquisition values, which leaves this path's
        rendering byte-identical to LOI_Path's while giving LOI_Path_Disposition the correct
        sequence. Appending was sufficient only because the two sets are FULLY DISJOINT - a
        value a record type does not expose cannot occupy a position in that record type's
        rendering. Contrast NDA__c, whose sets overlap: Not Sent had to be moved AHEAD of Sent
        in the master ordering because appending rendered the disposition STARTING state third.
        Anyone re-ordering Stage__c must re-check BOTH LOI record type files.

        Received, Under Review, Countered by DPEG, Counter Received from Buyer and Executed do
        not appear here because the Acquisition_LOI record type restricts them out; they belong
        to LOI_Path_Disposition.

        THE APPROVAL HOP LIVES AT Prepare/Review AND IS ACQUISITION-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION. The
        LOI__c.Submit_for_Approval quick action is gated on {!Record.Stage__c} EQUAL
        'Prepare/Review', a value Disposition_LOI does not carry, so it is self-limiting with no
        record-type criterion. Opportunity.LOI_Approval is a separate approval on the
        OPPORTUNITY object and has no relationship to an LOI__c record.

        POST-DEPLOY: this path renders nothing until every existing LOI row has been migrated
        off Master onto Acquisition_LOI and the record type is assigned in the profiles in use.

        =====================================================================
        🔴 AMENDED 2026-08-14 (Phase 3, observation 5). THE FIVE-STEP SEQUENCE AND THE
        "FULLY DISJOINT" PARAGRAPH ABOVE ARE BOTH SUPERSEDED. THIS FILE IS CURRENTLY IN A
        TRANSITIONAL NINE-STEP STATE ON PURPOSE.
        =====================================================================

        RETRACTED (1), verbatim: "Five steps: Draft -> Prepare/Review -> Sent -> Counter ->
        Completed" and "EVERY <info> TEXT BELOW IS COPIED BYTE-IDENTICAL FROM LOI_Path... Nothing
        on the acquisition side is reworded, re-ordered, added or dropped by this tranche."
        The target buy-side sequence is now
        Draft -> Under Review -> Submitted -> Negotiation -> Signed. The byte-identical rule was a
        statement about Tranche 3B, not a standing property; the four <info> texts for the new
        steps below are NEW PROSE and are the first original wording in this file.

        RETRACTED (2), verbatim: "Appending was sufficient only because the two sets are FULLY
        DISJOINT - a value a record type does not expose cannot occupy a position in that record
        type's rendering."
        'Under Review' is now exposed by BOTH record types. See LOI__c/fields/Stage__c's own
        comment for the retraction and the ordering arithmetic.

        ✅ P3-D3 DONE. The nine-step transitional state described above is retired. The four
        legacy steps (Prepare/Review, Sent, Counter, Completed) are removed from this file and
        from the Acquisition_LOI record type, and the underlying values are deactivated on
        Stage__c (never deleted - decision O3). The five steps below are now the whole file:
        Draft -> Under Review -> Submitted -> Negotiation -> Signed. Applied via
        agent-output/p3-d3-retire-loi-psa/.

        ⚠ THE 'Submitted' STEP NAMES TWO BUTTONS BY LABEL, SO IT IS COUPLED TO quickActions/.
        Its <info> says "use Negotiation ... or Signed", which are the CURRENT <label> values of
        LOI__c.Mark_Countered and LOI__c.Mark_Completed, both relabelled in this same phase
        (runbook 1.8: Counter -> Negotiation, Completed -> Signed) because the convention on this
        object is label == target stage. The first draft of this step said "use Counter", naming a
        button that no longer exists under that name; it is corrected here. This prose RENDERS ON
        THE PATH in front of a user, so a label change in quickActions/ and this text must move
        together. Nothing automated checks the pair; a wrong button name deploys green.

        ⚠ THE APPROVAL HOP MOVED FROM 'Prepare/Review' TO 'Under Review', AND 'Under Review' IS
        SHARED WITH Disposition_LOI. The retracted paragraph's claim that the approval hop is
        "acquisition-only BY CONSTRUCTION" because the gating value is one Disposition_LOI does
        not carry is therefore no longer true of the gating value. It remains true of this PATH -
        a Path is per record type - but LOI_Record_Page's Submit_for_Approval entry is now
        genuinely shared and carries an open residual; see that file's comment.
    -->
    <active>true</active>
    <entityName>LOI__c</entityName>
    <fieldName>Stage__c</fieldName>
    <masterLabel>LOI Path Acquisition</masterLabel>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <info>Draft the letter of intent - capture the offer price, cap rate and key terms. Keep it here until the draft is ready for internal review.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Draft</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <info>Review the LOI internally and route it for approval. Validate the terms and refine based on feedback. Advance Stage stays locked here until the LOI reads Approved, so use Submit for Approval first.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Under Review</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <info>LOI submitted to the seller/broker. Awaiting their response - this is the branch point: use Negotiation if they come back with terms, or Signed if they accept as submitted.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Submitted</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <info>Negotiation stage. Track the back-and-forth with the seller - log each round in the Counter History and update the offer terms until both sides align.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Negotiation</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <info>LOI signed by both sides. This is the terminal stage for the buy-side LOI and it is what releases the deal into Under Contract (PSA).</info>
        <picklistValueName>Signed</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <recordTypeName>Acquisition_LOI</recordTypeName>
</PathAssistant>
